on\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Blast Crisis (CML-BC):\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormal/excessive bleeding, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts (MDS-EB) / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL):\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, bone pain\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Lymphoblastic Lymphoma:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, cellular immunodeficiency, somatic mutation\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia in Blast Crisis (CLL-BC):\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n  Favours second: abdominal pain or fullness, anemia, bleeding, bone pain, easy bruising\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) vs Chronic Myeloid Leukemia in Blast Crisis (CML-BC):\n  Favours first:  acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormal/excessive bleeding, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts (MDS-EB) / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant inheritance, somatic mutation\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) vs Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL):\n  Favours first:  acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, bone pain\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) vs Lymphoblastic Lymphoma:\n  Favours first:  acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, cellular immunodeficiency, chronic lymphatic leukemia\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) vs Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia in Blast Crisis (CLL-BC):\n  Favours first:  acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance, somatic mutation\n  Favours second: abdominal pain or fullness, anemia, bleeding, bone pain, easy bruising\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia in Blast Crisis (CML-BC) vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts (MDS-EB) / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormal/excessive bleeding, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia in Blast Crisis (CML-BC) vs Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormal/excessive bleeding,